#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Generate 200 premium Mathematics Q&A pairs — Upgrade H"""
import json, pathlib

OUT = pathlib.Path('/workspace/data_upgrades')
OUT.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)

pairs = [
    ("What is the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus?", "The FTC links differentiation and integration: (1) if F(x)=integral from a to x of f(t)dt then F'(x)=f(x); (2) integral from a to b of f(x)dx = F(b)-F(a) where F is any antiderivative of f. Differentiation and integration are inverse operations."),
    ("Prove that sqrt(2) is irrational.", "Assume sqrt(2)=p/q in lowest terms. Then 2q^2=p^2, so p^2 is even, so p is even; write p=2k. Then 2q^2=4k^2, so q^2=2k^2, so q is even — contradicting p/q being in lowest terms. Therefore sqrt(2) is irrational."),
    ("What is a group in abstract algebra?", "A group (G,.) is a set with a binary operation satisfying: closure, associativity, an identity element e with e.a=a.e=a, and inverses (for each a there exists a^(-1) with a.a^(-1)=e). Groups model symmetry and structure throughout mathematics."),
    ("State and explain Lagrange's theorem.", "If H is a subgroup of a finite group G, then |H| divides |G|. The cosets of H partition G into equal parts each of size |H|, so |G|=|H|*(number of cosets). Corollary: the order of any element divides |G|."),
    ("What is the difference between a ring and a field?", "A ring has two operations where addition forms an abelian group, multiplication is associative, and distributivity holds. A field is a commutative ring where every non-zero element has a multiplicative inverse. Z is a ring but not a field; Q, R, C are fields."),
    ("What is a vector space?", "A vector space over a field F is a set V with vector addition and scalar multiplication satisfying eight axioms: commutativity and associativity of addition, existence of zero and negatives, compatibility of scalar multiplication, and distributivity laws. Examples: R^n, function spaces, polynomials."),
    ("What is a basis of a vector space?", "A basis is a linearly independent spanning set. Every vector in V has a unique representation as a linear combination of basis vectors. The dimension of V is the cardinality of any basis — all bases of a finite-dimensional space have the same size."),
    ("What is an eigenvalue and eigenvector?", "For a linear map A: V->V, a non-zero vector v is an eigenvector with eigenvalue lambda if Av=lambda*v. Eigenvalues are roots of the characteristic polynomial det(A-lambda*I)=0. Eigenvectors reveal directions preserved (up to scaling) by the transformation."),
    ("State the Spectral Theorem.", "A real symmetric matrix is orthogonally diagonalisable: it has real eigenvalues and an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors. A=QDQ^T where Q is orthogonal and D diagonal. Generalises to self-adjoint operators on Hilbert spaces."),
    ("What is the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality?", "|<u,v>|^2 <= <u,u>*<v,v>, with equality iff u and v are linearly dependent. In R^n: (sum u_i*v_i)^2 <= (sum u_i^2)*(sum v_i^2). It underlies the triangle inequality for norms and is central in analysis, probability, and quantum mechanics."),
    ("What is a metric space?", "A metric space (X,d) has a distance function satisfying: d(x,y)>=0, d(x,y)=0 iff x=y, symmetry, and the triangle inequality d(x,z)<=d(x,y)+d(y,z). It formalises 'closeness' without coordinates."),
    ("What is compactness in topology?", "A topological space is compact if every open cover has a finite subcover. In R^n, by Heine-Borel, compactness is equivalent to being closed and bounded. Continuous functions on compact spaces attain their extrema."),
    ("What is the Intermediate Value Theorem?", "If f is continuous on [a,b] and k is between f(a) and f(b), then there exists c in (a,b) with f(c)=k. Any continuous function that changes sign on an interval has a zero there — the basis of many existence proofs and root-finding algorithms."),
    ("What is the Mean Value Theorem?", "If f is continuous on [a,b] and differentiable on (a,b), there exists c in (a,b) with f'(c)=(f(b)-f(a))/(b-a). There is a point where the tangent is parallel to the secant. It underlies L'Hopital's rule and monotonicity criteria."),
    ("What is Taylor's theorem?", "A smooth function f can be approximated near a by a polynomial: f(x)=f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)+f''(a)(x-a)^2/2!+...+f^(n)(a)(x-a)^n/n!+R_n. The infinite series is the Taylor series; when it converges to f, f is called analytic."),
    ("What is the Fourier series?", "A periodic function f with period 2*pi can be written as a_0/2+sum(a_n*cos(n*x)+b_n*sin(n*x)) where the coefficients are computed by integrating f against cos and sin. Fourier series decompose signals into frequency components and are fundamental in analysis and signal processing."),
    ("What is a Hilbert space?", "A Hilbert space is a complete inner product space — a vector space with an inner product inducing a norm under which every Cauchy sequence converges. Examples: L^2(R), l^2, C^n. They are the natural setting for quantum mechanics and functional analysis."),
    ("What is the Banach fixed point theorem?", "If T is a contraction mapping on a complete metric space X (d(Tx,Ty)<=k*d(x,y) for some k<1), then T has a unique fixed point x*=Tx*, and iterating T from any starting point converges to x*. It is the basis of existence-uniqueness proofs in differential equations."),
    ("What is topology?", "Topology studies properties preserved under continuous deformations. A topological space specifies which subsets are open, satisfying: empty set and X are open; arbitrary unions of opens are open; finite intersections are open. Key concepts: continuity, compactness, connectedness, homeomorphism."),
    ("What is the Euler characteristic?", "For a convex polyhedron, V-E+F=2 (vertices minus edges plus faces). More generally, the Euler characteristic chi(X) is a topological invariant: chi=2 for a sphere, chi=0 for a torus, chi=2-2g for a surface of genus g."),
    ("What is the Riemann hypothesis?", "The non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function zeta(s)=sum n^(-s) all have real part 1/2. Proved for the first 10^13 zeros but unproven in general. Its truth would imply the prime counting function pi(x) fluctuates around Li(x) within the tightest possible bounds."),
    ("What is the prime number theorem?", "pi(x) ~ x/ln(x) as x->infinity, where pi(x) counts primes up to x. The n-th prime is approximately n*ln(n). Proved by Hadamard and de la Vallee Poussin in 1896 using complex analysis and properties of the Riemann zeta function."),
    ("What is modular arithmetic?", "Modular arithmetic considers integers modulo n: a is congruent to b mod n if n divides (a-b). It forms a ring Z/nZ; if n is prime, a field. Fundamental to cryptography (RSA), hashing, and number theory. The Chinese Remainder Theorem reconstructs values from their residues modulo coprime moduli."),
    ("State Fermat's little theorem.", "If p is prime and gcd(a,p)=1, then a^(p-1) is congruent to 1 mod p. It underlies RSA encryption and probabilistic primality tests. Euler's theorem generalises it: a^phi(n) is congruent to 1 mod n when gcd(a,n)=1."),
    ("What is the RSA algorithm's mathematical basis?", "RSA relies on: it is easy to multiply large primes p,q to get n=p*q but computationally hard to factor n. By Euler's theorem, m^phi(n) is congruent to 1 mod n. Choose e with gcd(e,phi(n))=1 and d=e^(-1) mod phi(n). Then (m^e)^d = m (mod n) — encryption and decryption are inverses."),
    ("What is a continuous function?", "f:X->Y is continuous at x if for every epsilon>0 there exists delta>0 such that d(x,y)<delta implies d(f(x),f(y))<epsilon. Equivalently, preimages of open sets are open. Continuity preserves convergence."),
    ("What is uniform continuity?", "f is uniformly continuous on X if a single delta works for all x: for every epsilon>0 there exists delta>0 such that d(x,y)<delta implies d(f(x),f(y))<epsilon for all x,y. Every continuous function on a compact set is uniformly continuous (Heine-Cantor)."),
    ("What is a power series?", "A power series sum a_n*(x-c)^n converges for |x-c|<R (radius of convergence) and diverges for |x-c|>R. Within its radius, it defines an analytic function differentiable term by term. The radius R=1/limsup|a_n|^(1/n)."),
    ("What is complex analysis?", "Complex analysis studies functions f:C->C. A function is holomorphic (complex differentiable) at z if the derivative limit exists in C. Holomorphic functions satisfy the Cauchy-Riemann equations, are infinitely differentiable, and have remarkable rigidity absent in real analysis."),
    ("State Cauchy's integral theorem.", "If f is holomorphic on a simply connected domain D and gamma is a closed curve in D, then the contour integral of f around gamma equals zero. Consequence: holomorphic functions are path-independent; Cauchy's integral formula gives f(a) as (1/2*pi*i) times the integral of f(z)/(z-a)."),
    ("What is the residue theorem?", "If f is meromorphic inside a closed curve gamma with poles at z_1,...,z_n, then the contour integral equals 2*pi*i times the sum of residues. The residue at an isolated singularity is the coefficient of 1/(z-z_0) in the Laurent expansion. Used for evaluating real definite integrals."),
    ("What is a differential equation?", "A differential equation relates a function to its derivatives. ODEs involve functions of one variable; PDEs involve partial derivatives. They model physical, biological, and economic systems. Key concepts: order, linearity, existence and uniqueness of solutions, stability."),
    ("State the existence and uniqueness theorem for ODEs.", "If f(t,y) is continuous and Lipschitz in y on a rectangle around (t_0,y_0), then the IVP y'=f(t,y), y(t_0)=y_0 has a unique solution on some interval containing t_0. Proved via Picard iteration and the Banach fixed point theorem."),
    ("What is a Fourier transform?", "F_hat(omega)=integral of f(t)*e^(-2*pi*i*omega*t)dt. It decomposes a function into frequency components. Key properties: linearity, convolution becomes multiplication, differentiation becomes multiplication by 2*pi*i*omega. Central in signal processing, PDEs, and quantum mechanics."),
    ("What is the convolution theorem?", "The Fourier transform of the convolution (f*g)(t) is the pointwise product F_hat(f)*F_hat(g). This converts convolution (expensive in time domain) to multiplication in frequency domain — the basis of fast filtering and the FFT algorithm."),
    ("What is Lebesgue integration?", "Lebesgue integration partitions the range of f rather than the domain. It integrates a much wider class of functions than Riemann integration and satisfies powerful convergence theorems (monotone convergence, dominated convergence) essential in modern analysis and probability."),
    ("What is measure theory?", "Measure theory formalises length, area, probability, and integration. A measure mu on a sigma-algebra assigns non-negative values to measurable sets satisfying countable additivity. Lebesgue measure on R extends length; probability measures have total measure 1."),
    ("What is a probability space?", "A probability space (Omega,F,P) consists of a sample space Omega, a sigma-algebra F of events, and a probability measure P with P(Omega)=1. Random variables are measurable functions X:Omega->R. Expectation is E[X]=integral of X dP."),
    ("State the law of large numbers.", "Weak LLN: if X_1,X_2,... are i.i.d. with mean mu, then the sample average converges to mu in probability. Strong LLN: convergence is almost sure. This grounds frequentist probability — sample averages converge to population means."),
    ("State the central limit theorem.", "If X_1,...,X_n are i.i.d. with mean mu and variance sigma^2, then (X_bar - mu)/(sigma/sqrt(n)) converges in distribution to N(0,1). The sum of many independent random variables tends to be normally distributed regardless of their individual distributions."),
    ("What is Bayes' theorem?", "P(A|B)=P(B|A)*P(A)/P(B). It updates prior probability P(A) to posterior P(A|B) given evidence B. The likelihood P(B|A) measures how well A explains B. Fundamental in statistics, machine learning, and probabilistic reasoning."),
    ("What is a Markov chain?", "A Markov chain is a sequence where P(X_{n+1}=s|X_1,...,X_n)=P(X_{n+1}=s|X_n) — future depends only on the present state. Described by a transition matrix. Stationary distributions satisfy pi*P=pi; ergodic chains converge to their stationary distribution."),
    ("What is the Perron-Frobenius theorem?", "A matrix with strictly positive entries has a unique largest real eigenvalue (the Perron root) with a strictly positive eigenvector (the Perron vector). It ensures Markov chains with positive transition matrices have a unique stationary distribution and governs convergence to it."),
    ("What is graph theory?", "Graph theory studies graphs G=(V,E) — sets of vertices and edges. Key concepts: degree, connectivity, paths, cycles, trees, planarity, colouring, matching. Applications span computer networks, social networks, scheduling, and algorithm design."),
    ("State the four colour theorem.", "Any planar map can be properly coloured with at most four colours so that no adjacent regions share a colour. Proved by Appel and Haken (1976) using extensive computer case analysis — the first major theorem proven computationally."),
    ("What is Euler's formula for graphs?", "For a connected planar graph: V-E+F=2 (vertices, edges, faces including the outer face). Consequence: any planar graph satisfies E<=3V-6 for V>=3 — used to prove K_5 and K_{3,3} are non-planar (Kuratowski's theorem)."),
    ("What is the halting problem?", "Turing proved no algorithm can decide whether an arbitrary program halts on a given input. The proof uses diagonalisation: suppose a halter H exists; construct program D that halts iff H says it doesn't — contradiction. Many problems reduce to it."),
    ("What is Godel's first incompleteness theorem?", "In any consistent formal system strong enough to express arithmetic, there exist true statements that cannot be proved within the system. Godel constructed a statement G that asserts its own unprovability — if provable it would be false, so it's true but unprovable."),
    ("What is the P vs NP problem?", "P is the class of problems solvable in polynomial time; NP is the class where solutions can be verified in polynomial time. P is contained in NP trivially. Whether P=NP — whether every verifiable problem is also efficiently solvable — is the central open problem of computer science."),
    ("What is a topological space?", "A topological space (X,tau) is a set X with a collection tau of open sets such that the empty set and X are in tau, arbitrary unions of opens are open, and finite intersections are open. It generalises metric spaces to study continuity and convergence abstractly."),
    ("What is a manifold?", "An n-dimensional manifold is a topological space locally homeomorphic to R^n. Smooth manifolds additionally have compatible smooth coordinate charts, allowing calculus. Examples: curves (1-manifolds), surfaces (2-manifolds), spacetime (4-manifold)."),
    ("What is a Lie group?", "A Lie group is a group that is also a smooth manifold, with smooth group operations. Examples: GL(n,R), SO(n), SU(n). Lie groups model continuous symmetries; their Lie algebras (tangent space at the identity) classify them and appear throughout physics."),
    ("What is differential geometry?", "Differential geometry studies smooth manifolds using calculus. Key objects: tangent vectors, vector fields, differential forms, connections, curvature. The Gauss-Bonnet theorem links geometry to topology. General relativity is formulated in differential geometry."),
    ("State Stokes' theorem.", "The integral of a differential k-form omega over the boundary of an oriented manifold equals the integral of its exterior derivative d*omega over the manifold. Special cases: Green's theorem, divergence theorem, classical Stokes theorem. It unifies all integration theorems of vector calculus."),
    ("What is the Gauss-Bonnet theorem?", "For a compact Riemannian surface, the integral of Gaussian curvature plus the integral of geodesic curvature on the boundary equals 2*pi times the Euler characteristic. Total curvature is a topological invariant — independent of the specific geometry of the surface."),
    ("What is curvature?", "For a curve in R^2, curvature kappa measures how sharply the unit tangent turns per unit arclength. For surfaces, Gaussian curvature K=kappa_1*kappa_2 (product of principal curvatures) is intrinsic (Theorema Egregium). The Riemann curvature tensor generalises this to manifolds."),
    ("What is a category?", "A category consists of objects and morphisms (arrows) satisfying: each object has an identity morphism, morphisms compose associatively. Examples: sets with functions, groups with homomorphisms. Category theory studies structure-preserving maps at the highest level of abstraction."),
    ("What is a functor?", "A functor F:C->D maps objects to objects and morphisms to morphisms, preserving identity and composition. Functors express structural relationships between categories. Examples: fundamental group pi_1 (topology to groups), homology, forgetful functors."),
    ("What is a natural transformation?", "A natural transformation eta:F=>G between functors assigns to each object A a morphism eta_A:F(A)->G(A) compatible with all morphisms in C. It is a 'morphism between functors' and makes precise the notion of a 'natural' construction."),
    ("What is algebraic topology?", "Algebraic topology assigns algebraic invariants (groups, rings) to topological spaces to distinguish them. Key tools: fundamental group pi_1, homology groups H_n, cohomology rings. Continuous maps induce group homomorphisms, making topology amenable to algebra."),
    ("What is the fundamental group?", "The fundamental group pi_1(X,x_0) consists of homotopy classes of loops based at x_0 under concatenation. It detects holes: pi_1(circle)=Z, pi_1(plane)=0, pi_1(torus)=Z*Z. The Seifert-van Kampen theorem computes it for spaces built from simpler pieces."),
    ("What is homology?", "Homology H_n(X) counts n-dimensional holes: H_0 counts connected components, H_1 counts 1-cycles (loops not bounding disks), H_2 counts 2-cycles. Defined via chain complexes and the boundary operator with d^2=0; H_n=ker(d_n)/im(d_{n+1})."),
    ("What is a linear programming problem?", "LP minimises a linear objective c^T*x subject to A*x<=b, x>=0. The feasible region is a polytope; the optimum occurs at a vertex. The simplex method traverses vertices; interior point methods offer polynomial complexity. LP is the foundation of operations research."),
    ("What is the simplex method?", "The simplex method solves LP by starting at a feasible vertex and moving along edges to adjacent vertices with better objective value until no improvement is possible. Though exponential in the worst case, it is efficient in practice. Dantzig developed it in 1947."),
    ("What is duality in linear programming?", "Every LP (primal) has a dual LP. Strong duality: if both are feasible, their optimal values are equal. Complementary slackness: at optimality, either a constraint is active or the corresponding dual variable is zero. Duality underlies shadow price interpretations."),
    ("What is convex optimisation?", "Convex optimisation minimises a convex objective over a convex feasible set. Every local minimum is global. Subfields: linear programming, quadratic programming, semidefinite programming. Interior point methods solve these in polynomial time."),
    ("What is gradient descent?", "Gradient descent updates parameters theta <- theta - alpha * gradient(L(theta)) to minimise a loss L. Stochastic gradient descent uses mini-batch gradient estimates. Convergence depends on learning rate and function geometry. Adaptive methods (Adam, RMSProp) adjust step sizes per parameter."),
    ("What is Newton's method?", "Newton's method finds roots by linearising: x_{n+1}=x_n - f(x_n)/f'(x_n). In optimisation: theta_{n+1}=theta_n - [Hessian]^(-1) * gradient. Quadratic convergence near a root makes it fast, but computing the Hessian is expensive for high dimensions."),
    ("What is MCMC?", "Markov chain Monte Carlo algorithms generate samples from a target distribution pi(x) by constructing a Markov chain with pi as its stationary distribution. Metropolis-Hastings and Gibbs sampling are canonical examples. Used in Bayesian posterior inference over high-dimensional parameters."),
    ("What is the law of total probability?", "If B_1,...,B_n partition the sample space, then P(A)=sum P(A|B_i)*P(B_i). It decomposes a probability over exhaustive cases and combined with Bayes' theorem enables computing posteriors from likelihoods and priors."),
    ("What is conditional expectation?", "E[X|Y] is the best predictor of X given Y in the L^2 sense; it is a function of Y. Tower property: E[E[X|Y]]=E[X]. Conditional expectation is central to martingale theory, Bayesian inference, and the analysis of stochastic processes."),
    ("What is a martingale?", "A process {X_n} is a martingale if E[X_{n+1}|X_1,...,X_n]=X_n — best prediction of future given present equals present. Cumulative sums of fair coin flips are martingales. Optional stopping: E[X_tau]=E[X_0] for bounded stopping times tau."),
    ("What is the Pythagorean theorem?", "In a right triangle with legs a, b and hypotenuse c: a^2+b^2=c^2. Proof by area: arrange four triangles around a square of side c; rearranging proves the identity. Generalised by the law of cosines and the inner product norm in Hilbert spaces."),
    ("What is the law of cosines?", "c^2=a^2+b^2-2*a*b*cos(C), where C is the angle opposite side c. It generalises Pythagoras (C=90 degrees gives a^2+b^2=c^2) and solves triangles given two sides and the included angle or three sides."),
    ("What is a complex number?", "A complex number z=a+bi where a,b are real and i^2=-1. Modulus |z|=sqrt(a^2+b^2); argument theta=arctan(b/a). Polar form: z=r*e^(i*theta). The complex numbers C are algebraically closed — every polynomial over C has a root."),
    ("State the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra.", "Every non-constant polynomial with complex coefficients has at least one complex root. Every degree-n polynomial over C factors into n linear factors (counting multiplicity). Proofs use topology (winding numbers) or Liouville's theorem from complex analysis."),
    ("What is Euler's identity?", "e^(i*pi)+1=0. It follows from Euler's formula e^(i*theta)=cos(theta)+i*sin(theta) at theta=pi. It unites the five most fundamental constants of mathematics (e, i, pi, 1, 0) in a single equation."),
    ("What are the conic sections?", "Conic sections are intersections of a cone with a plane: circle, ellipse, parabola, hyperbola. Their unified equation: Ax^2+Bxy+Cy^2+Dx+Ey+F=0. Discriminant B^2-4AC<0 (ellipse), =0 (parabola), >0 (hyperbola)."),
    ("What is the Gram-Schmidt process?", "Given a basis {v_1,...,v_n}, Gram-Schmidt produces an orthonormal basis by iteratively subtracting projections: u_k=v_k - sum of projections onto previous basis vectors, then normalise. Fundamental for QR decomposition and least-squares problems."),
    ("What is LU decomposition?", "LU decomposition factors A=LU where L is lower triangular and U is upper triangular (with partial pivoting: PA=LU). Solving Ax=b becomes two triangular systems solvable in O(n^2). Used for linear systems, determinants, and matrix inversion."),
    ("What is singular value decomposition?", "Every real m*n matrix A=U*Sigma*V^T where U and V are orthogonal and Sigma is diagonal with non-negative singular values in decreasing order. SVD reveals rank, condition number, and best low-rank approximations (Eckart-Young theorem). Used in PCA, LSI, and image compression."),
    ("What is the rank-nullity theorem?", "For a linear map T:V->W: rank(T)+nullity(T)=dim(V), where rank=dim(image) and nullity=dim(kernel). Knowing one dimension determines the other given dim(V)."),
    ("What is a determinant?", "The determinant det(A) measures the signed volume scaling of the linear map A. det(AB)=det(A)*det(B); det(A)=0 iff A is singular. Computed via cofactor expansion or row reduction. det(A) equals the product of all eigenvalues."),
    ("What is Jordan normal form?", "Every complex matrix is similar to a Jordan matrix with eigenvalues on the diagonal and 1s on the superdiagonal in Jordan blocks. It completely classifies linear operators over C up to similarity and is used in solving linear ODEs."),
    ("What is the Cayley-Hamilton theorem?", "Every square matrix satisfies its own characteristic polynomial: if p(lambda)=det(A-lambda*I), then p(A)=0. This allows expressing A^k for k>=n as a linear combination of I,A,...,A^(n-1) and is used in computing matrix functions."),
    ("What is mathematical induction?", "Induction proves P(n) for all n>=n_0 in two steps: Base case: prove P(n_0). Inductive step: assume P(k) and prove P(k+1). Strong induction assumes all P(n_0),...,P(k). Equivalent to the well-ordering principle: every non-empty set of natural numbers has a least element."),
    ("What is combinatorics?", "Combinatorics counts, arranges, and analyses discrete structures. Key formulas: permutations P(n,k)=n!/(n-k)!, combinations C(n,k)=n!/(k!*(n-k)!), inclusion-exclusion, generating functions. Applications: probability, algorithm analysis, coding theory, graph theory."),
    ("What is the inclusion-exclusion principle?", "|A_1 union ... union A_n| = sum|A_i| - sum|A_i intersect A_j| + ... The alternating sum counts elements appearing in at least one set. Applications: counting derangements, Euler's totient function, chromatic polynomials."),
    ("What is a generating function?", "A generating function encodes a sequence {a_n} as a formal power series A(x)=sum a_n*x^n. Operations on series correspond to combinatorial operations. Ordinary GFs count unlabelled structures; exponential GFs count labelled structures. Solve recurrences by algebraic manipulation."),
    ("What is the pigeonhole principle?", "If n+1 objects are placed into n boxes, some box contains at least two objects. Generalised: if N objects in k boxes, some box contains at least ceil(N/k). Used to prove: any sequence of n^2+1 distinct numbers has a monotone subsequence of length n+1."),
    ("What is Ramsey theory?", "Ramsey theory proves that sufficiently large structures must contain ordered substructures. R(s,t) is the smallest n such that any 2-colouring of edges of K_n contains a red K_s or blue K_t. R(3,3)=6: any 6 people include 3 mutual friends or 3 mutual strangers."),
    ("What is a prime number?", "A prime p>1 has no divisors other than 1 and itself. Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic: every integer >1 factors uniquely into primes. There are infinitely many primes (Euclid: suppose finitely many p_1,...,p_n; then p_1*...*p_n+1 is divisible by none of them)."),
    ("What is the sieve of Eratosthenes?", "To find all primes up to N: list 2,3,...,N; starting from p=2, strike out all multiples; advance to the next un-struck number and repeat. Complexity O(N log log N). Basis for more sophisticated analytic sieves."),
    ("What is Diophantine analysis?", "Diophantine equations seek integer solutions. Linear: ax+by=c has solutions iff gcd(a,b) divides c (Bezout's lemma). Pythagorean triples: x^2+y^2=z^2. Fermat's Last Theorem (Wiles 1995): x^n+y^n=z^n has no positive integer solutions for n>=3."),
    ("What is Galois theory?", "Galois theory connects field extensions to group theory via the Galois correspondence: subgroups of Gal(K/F) correspond bijectively to intermediate fields. The polynomial f is solvable by radicals iff its Galois group is solvable. This proves quintics are generally not solvable by radicals."),
    ("What is the structure theorem for finitely generated abelian groups?", "Every finitely generated abelian group is isomorphic to Z^r * Z/d_1*Z * ... * Z/d_k*Z where d_1|d_2|...|d_k (invariant factor form) or equivalently the primary decomposition. It classifies finite abelian groups completely."),
    ("What is functional analysis?", "Functional analysis studies infinite-dimensional vector spaces (function spaces) and operators on them. Key spaces: Banach (complete normed), Hilbert (complete inner product). Key theorems: Hahn-Banach, open mapping, closed graph, uniform boundedness (Banach-Steinhaus)."),
    ("What is the Hahn-Banach theorem?", "A bounded linear functional on a subspace extends to the whole normed space with the same norm. It guarantees the existence of enough continuous linear functionals to separate points and is fundamental for duality theory in functional analysis."),
    ("What is spectral theory?", "Spectral theory generalises eigenvalues to infinite-dimensional operators. The spectrum sigma(T) is the set of lambda for which T-lambda*I is not invertible. For self-adjoint operators the spectrum is real and the spectral theorem provides an integral decomposition analogous to diagonalisation."),
    ("What is a Sobolev space?", "Sobolev space W^{k,p}(Omega) consists of functions in L^p(Omega) whose weak derivatives up to order k are also in L^p. They provide the natural setting for PDEs (existence, regularity, trace theorems) and are central in modern PDE theory and numerical analysis."),
    ("What is the heat equation?", "du/dt = alpha * Laplacian(u) models diffusion of heat or particles. Solution via Fourier series on bounded domains; via the Gaussian heat kernel on R^n: u(x,t)=integral of G(x-y,t)*u_0(y)dy. The maximum principle and parabolic regularity theory are key tools."),
    ("What is the wave equation?", "d^2u/dt^2 = c^2 * Laplacian(u) models acoustic, electromagnetic, and elastic waves. D'Alembert solution in 1D: u(x,t)=f(x+ct)+g(x-ct). Huygens' principle in 3D: disturbances travel on the light cone, not inside it."),
    ("What is information theory?", "Shannon's information theory quantifies information. Entropy H(X)=-sum p(x)*log p(x) measures uncertainty. Channel capacity C=max mutual information is the maximum reliable communication rate. Shannon's theorem: reliable communication is possible iff rate < C."),
    ("What is Shannon entropy?", "H(X)=-sum p_i * log p_i (bits if log base 2). It measures the average information content of a random variable X. H=0 for a certain outcome; H=log(n) for a uniform distribution over n outcomes — the maximum entropy distribution."),
    ("What is Kolmogorov complexity?", "The Kolmogorov complexity K(x) of a string x is the length of the shortest program that outputs x on a universal Turing machine. It formalises algorithmic randomness: a string is random if K(x) is approximately |x| — no shorter description exists."),
    ("What is the master theorem for recurrences?", "For T(n)=a*T(n/b)+f(n) with a>=1, b>1: let c=log_b(a). If f(n)=O(n^{c-epsilon}), T=Theta(n^c); if f(n)=Theta(n^c * log^k(n)), T=Theta(n^c * log^{k+1}(n)); if f(n)=Omega(n^{c+epsilon}) and regularity holds, T=Theta(f(n)). Quickly solves divide-and-conquer recurrences."),
    ("What is the Riemann zeta function?", "zeta(s)=sum_{n=1}^{inf} n^{-s} for Re(s)>1. Euler product: zeta(s)=product over primes p of (1-p^{-s})^{-1} — encodes prime distribution. Analytically continued to all s (except s=1). Its zeros control prime counting oscillations; the Riemann hypothesis concerns their location."),
    ("What is the Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem?", "Every bounded sequence in R^n has a convergent subsequence. In R, a bounded sequence has a monotone subsequence (by a peak point argument), which converges. Equivalent to completeness of R and compactness of closed bounded sets."),
    ("What is a sigma-algebra?", "A sigma-algebra F on X is a collection of subsets closed under complement and countable unions. It defines what sets are measurable. The Borel sigma-algebra on R is generated by open sets; the Lebesgue sigma-algebra extends it to include null sets."),
    ("What is the Radon-Nikodym theorem?", "If nu is absolutely continuous with respect to mu (mu(A)=0 implies nu(A)=0), then there exists a measurable function f (the Radon-Nikodym derivative dnu/dmu) such that nu(A)=integral over A of f dmu. It grounds conditional expectation in measure theory."),
    ("What is dominated convergence theorem?", "If f_n converges to f pointwise and |f_n|<=g for an integrable g, then the integral of f_n converges to the integral of f. It permits interchanging limit and integral and is crucial in analysis and probability theory."),
    ("What is an L^p space?", "L^p(Omega,mu) consists of measurable functions with finite p-norm: (integral |f|^p dmu)^(1/p)<inf. L^2 is a Hilbert space. Holder's inequality: norm(fg,1)<=norm(f,p)*norm(g,q) where 1/p+1/q=1. These spaces unify analysis across measure theory."),
    ("What is the Stone-Weierstrass theorem?", "If A is a subalgebra of C(K) (continuous functions on compact K) that separates points and contains constants, then A is dense in C(K) in the uniform norm. The classical Weierstrass theorem (polynomials are dense in C[a,b]) is a special case."),
    ("What is maximum likelihood estimation?", "MLE finds parameters theta that maximise the likelihood L(theta)=P(data|theta). Under regularity conditions, MLE is consistent, asymptotically normal, and achieves the Cramer-Rao lower bound asymptotically — it is asymptotically efficient."),
    ("What is the Cramer-Rao bound?", "For any unbiased estimator T of theta: Var(T) >= 1/I(theta), where I(theta) is the Fisher information. The bound quantifies the minimum variance any unbiased estimator can achieve. MLE achieves it asymptotically."),
    ("What is regularisation in statistics?", "Regularisation adds a penalty to the loss to prevent overfitting. Ridge (L2): penalty lambda*sum(theta_i^2) shrinks coefficients; LASSO (L1): penalty lambda*sum(|theta_i|) produces sparsity. Bayesian interpretation: regularisation corresponds to a prior on parameters."),
    ("What is the bias-variance tradeoff?", "MSE of an estimator = Bias^2 + Variance + Noise. High-complexity models have low bias but high variance (overfit); low-complexity models have high bias but low variance (underfit). Regularisation, cross-validation, and ensemble methods manage this tradeoff."),
    ("What is principal component analysis?", "PCA finds directions of maximum variance via eigendecomposition of the covariance matrix. Projecting onto the top k eigenvectors gives the best k-dimensional linear approximation by MSE. Equivalent to the top-k SVD of the centred data matrix."),
    ("What is the expectation-maximisation algorithm?", "EM maximises likelihood when data has latent variables. E-step: compute expected complete-data log-likelihood given current parameters. M-step: maximise it. Iterating increases likelihood monotonically. Used in mixture models, HMMs, and imputation."),
    ("What is the kernel trick in machine learning?", "Many algorithms depend on data only through inner products. Replacing the inner product with a kernel k(x,y)=<phi(x),phi(y)> implicitly maps data to a high-dimensional feature space. By Mercer's theorem, any positive semi-definite symmetric function is a valid kernel."),
    ("What is a support vector machine?", "SVM finds the maximum-margin hyperplane separating classes. The dual involves only inner products — replaced by a kernel for non-linear separation. The support vectors are training points on the margin boundary; only they determine the solution."),
    ("What is a Gaussian process?", "A Gaussian process is a distribution over functions such that any finite collection of function values has a joint Gaussian distribution. Characterised by mean and covariance kernel. GP regression provides a principled Bayesian non-parametric regression with calibrated uncertainty."),
    ("What is optimal transport?", "Optimal transport finds the minimum-cost way to move mass from one distribution to another. The Wasserstein distance W_p(mu,nu) is the p-th root of the minimum expected distance^p over all joint distributions with marginals mu and nu. Used in generative models, statistics, and economics."),
    ("What is variational inference?", "Variational inference approximates an intractable posterior p(z|x) by optimising over a tractable family Q: minimise KL divergence from q(z) to p(z|x). Equivalently, maximise the ELBO. Mean-field VI assumes q factorises across coordinates. Used at scale in Bayesian deep learning."),
    ("What is topological data analysis?", "TDA applies algebraic topology to data. Persistent homology tracks how topological features (components, loops, voids) appear and disappear as a scale parameter grows. The persistence diagram summarises multi-scale topology. It is robust to noise and used for shape analysis."),
    ("What is symplectic geometry?", "Symplectic geometry studies manifolds with a closed non-degenerate 2-form omega. It is the natural framework for Hamiltonian mechanics: Hamilton's equations follow from the symplectic structure. Liouville's theorem states Hamiltonian flows preserve phase space volume."),
    ("What is the modularity theorem?", "Every elliptic curve over Q is modular — its L-function equals the L-function of a modular form. Proved by Wiles (semi-stable case, 1995), completed in 2001. It implied Fermat's Last Theorem as a consequence of Ribet's earlier theorem."),
    ("What is the Langlands program?", "A vast network of conjectures linking number theory (L-functions, Galois representations), automorphic forms, and representation theory. Wiles' proof of Fermat's Last Theorem proved a special case. The full program is the central organising theme of modern number theory."),
    ("What is p-adic analysis?", "The p-adic numbers Q_p complete Q with respect to the p-adic norm |p^a * m/n|_p = p^(-a). Large powers of p become small — the geometry is ultrametric. Applications in number theory (local-global principles), arithmetic geometry, and physics."),
    ("What is information geometry?", "Information geometry applies differential geometry to statistical manifolds — spaces of probability distributions. The Fisher information matrix is a Riemannian metric. Geodesics and dual connections give geometric insight into statistical inference and information theory."),
    ("What is the Atiyah-Singer index theorem?", "The analytical index of an elliptic differential operator D on a compact manifold (dim ker D - dim coker D) equals the topological index computed from characteristic classes. It unifies Gauss-Bonnet, Riemann-Roch, and Hirzebruch's signature theorem."),
    ("What is category theory's role in foundations?", "Category theory provides an alternative to set-theoretic foundations, describing structures by their morphisms rather than elements. Higher category theory and homotopy type theory suggest foundations where identity is inherently homotopy-theoretic rather than purely set-theoretic."),
    ("What is gradient flow in optimisation?", "Gradient flow solves d_theta/dt=-gradient(L(theta)) in continuous time — the ODE version of gradient descent. In Wasserstein space, diffusion processes are gradient flows of the KL divergence. Gradient flows unify optimisation and sampling in a geometric framework."),
    ("What is a sheaf in algebraic geometry?", "A sheaf on a topological space assigns data (groups, rings) to open sets with compatible restriction maps, satisfying a gluing axiom. Sheaf theory is the language of modern algebraic geometry, enabling local-to-global arguments and the definition of cohomology in algebraic settings."),
    ("What is K-theory?", "K-theory assigns groups K_n(X) to topological spaces or rings, encoding information about vector bundles (topological K-theory) or projective modules (algebraic K-theory). The Atiyah-Singer index theorem is formulated and proved using K-theory."),
    ("What is Brownian motion?", "Standard Brownian motion B(t) satisfies: B(0)=0, independent and stationary increments, B(t)-B(s)~N(0,t-s), and continuous sample paths almost surely. It models particle diffusion, stock prices (Black-Scholes), and is the limit of rescaled random walks (Donsker's theorem)."),
    ("What is the Ito integral?", "The Ito integral integrates adapted processes against Brownian motion. Ito's formula: df(B(t))=f'(B)dB + 1/2*f''(B)dt — the correction term 1/2*f''*dt arises from Brownian motion's quadratic variation [B]_t=t. This underpins stochastic calculus and mathematical finance."),
    ("What is the Black-Scholes formula?", "Under risk-neutral dynamics, the European call price is C=S_0*N(d1)-K*e^(-rT)*N(d2), where d1=(ln(S_0/K)+(r+sigma^2/2)*T)/(sigma*sqrt(T)) and d2=d1-sigma*sqrt(T). Derived via Ito's formula and no-arbitrage. Won Scholes and Merton the 1997 Nobel Prize in Economics."),
    ("What is the Pythagorean triple formula?", "All primitive Pythagorean triples (a^2+b^2=c^2 with gcd(a,b,c)=1) are given by a=m^2-n^2, b=2mn, c=m^2+n^2 for integers m>n>0 with gcd(m,n)=1 and m-n odd. This parametrisation is proved using Gaussian integers — unique factorisation in Z[i]."),
    ("What is the Chinese Remainder Theorem?", "If m_1,...,m_k are pairwise coprime, the system x is congruent to a_i (mod m_i) has a unique solution modulo M=product of m_i. It establishes Z/MZ is isomorphic to Z/m_1*Z * ... * Z/m_k*Z. Used in cryptography (RSA with CRT speedup) and computational number theory."),
    ("What is Bezout's identity?", "For integers a and b with gcd(a,b)=d, there exist integers x and y such that a*x+b*y=d. The extended Euclidean algorithm computes x and y. This is fundamental for modular inverses: if gcd(a,n)=1, then a has a multiplicative inverse mod n given by x in a*x+n*y=1."),
    ("What is the quadratic formula?", "The roots of ax^2+bx+c=0 are x=(-b +/- sqrt(b^2-4ac))/(2a). The discriminant b^2-4ac determines the nature: positive gives two distinct real roots, zero gives a repeated real root, negative gives two complex conjugate roots. Derived by completing the square."),
    ("What is completing the square?", "Completing the square rewrites ax^2+bx+c as a*(x+b/(2a))^2-(b^2-4ac)/(4a). It solves quadratics, derives the quadratic formula, transforms conic sections to standard form, and underpins Gaussian integral evaluation: integral of e^(-ax^2+bx)dx = sqrt(pi/a)*e^(b^2/4a)."),
    ("What is the geometric series formula?", "For |r|<1: sum_{n=0}^{inf} r^n = 1/(1-r). Finite geometric series: sum_{n=0}^{N-1} r^n = (1-r^N)/(1-r). The formula underpins compound interest calculations, infinite series convergence, and generating function manipulations."),
    ("What is the binomial theorem?", "(x+y)^n = sum_{k=0}^{n} C(n,k) * x^{n-k} * y^k where C(n,k)=n!/(k!*(n-k)!) are binomial coefficients. These appear in Pascal's triangle, combinatorics, and probability. The generalised binomial theorem extends this to non-integer exponents via infinite series."),
    ("What is a Poisson distribution?", "The Poisson distribution P(X=k)=lambda^k * e^{-lambda}/k! models the number of rare events in a fixed interval when events occur independently at mean rate lambda. It arises as the limit of Binomial(n,p) as n->inf, p->0 with n*p=lambda."),
    ("What is a normal distribution?", "The normal (Gaussian) distribution N(mu,sigma^2) has PDF (1/(sigma*sqrt(2pi)))*e^{-(x-mu)^2/(2*sigma^2)}. It arises from the CLT, maximises entropy for fixed mean and variance, and is characterised by its first two moments. The standard normal N(0,1) has CDF denoted Phi."),
    ("What is the exponential distribution?", "The exponential distribution Exp(lambda) has PDF lambda*e^{-lambda*x} for x>=0. It models inter-arrival times in a Poisson process and is the unique continuous memoryless distribution: P(X>s+t|X>s)=P(X>t). Mean=1/lambda; variance=1/lambda^2."),
    ("What is moment generating function?", "The MGF M_X(t)=E[e^{tX}] encodes all moments: E[X^n]=M^{(n)}_X(0). If two distributions have the same MGF in a neighbourhood of 0, they are identical. MGFs are used to prove CLT and to compute distributions of sums of independent random variables."),
    ("What is characteristic function in probability?", "The characteristic function phi_X(t)=E[e^{itX}] always exists (unlike MGF). It uniquely determines the distribution. phi_{X+Y}(t)=phi_X(t)*phi_Y(t) for independent X,Y. The Fourier inversion recovers the density. It is the main tool for proving convergence in distribution results."),
]

